  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 6.3. Increased capacity of Member States to develop gender-responsive legislation, policies and measures for a world of work free from violence and harassment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Department of Labour and Occupational Safety prepared and validated its action plan on strengthening action against violence and harassment in the world of work in Nepal in June 2022 through tripartite consultation. Under the action plan, a model code of conduct was developed and operationalized through sectoral codes of conduct for informal sectors particularly prone to or vulnerable to violence and harassment, with clear information on reporting, remedy, and enforcement mechanisms. As per the action plan, so far, the awareness of tripartite constituents has been raised by integrating issues related to violence and harassment into various trainings and capacitation programs.

The Federation of Nepalese Chambers of Commerce and Industry organised, developed and endorsed an online course on C190,which will be available for all its members by the end of 2023.

Following nationwide consultations, the Federation of Nepali Journalists developed and endorsed guidelines on grievance handling mechanisms and a model code of conduct for media houses in July 2022. The guideline includes the concept of zero tolerance in the mass media sector, adopting a broader definition of &#x2018;workplace&#x2019; and &#x2018;violence and harassment&#x2019;. The guideline has sections on protection and prevention, including managers&#x2019; responsibilities, and the provision of training courses on violence and harassment. It also has sections on complaints and hearings, the formation of a grievance hearing committee, the protection of the complainant, and grievance hearing procedures. The final section is on inspection, monitoring, and action. ILO provided expert advice and support to the Department of Labour and Occupational Safety to organise a tripartite strategic planning workshop in June 2022 and a provincial tripartite consultation in September 2022.
ILO entered into an implementation agreement with an organisation that has expertise in training development. ILO, together with a training organisation, organised consultation meetings with FNCCI in 2022 and 2023 for the finalisation of training modules in line with ILO Convention No. 190 and Recommendation No. 206.
ILO provided advisory services to the media guidelines on safety drafting committee  and shared the ILO convention on C190, Recommendation 205, and other good practices from other countries.</narrative>
   </description>
