   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 6.1. Increased capacity of Member States to promote investments in the care economy and a more balanced sharing of family responsibilities</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government of Nepal in collaboration with UN Women and ILO took several measures to collect evidence from assessments through the formation of a tripartite National Steering Committee on women&apos;s decent employment and investments in the care economy. The Government represented by the National Planning Commission (NPC) took the leadership for formulating policy recommendations on care to be integrated within the 16th periodic plan. Global policy tools &#x2018;Assessing the gendered employment impacts of COVID-19 and supporting a gender-responsive recovery&#x2019; (Link: Government&#x2019;s leadership. The gendered employment impacts and costing and economic returns of public investment in education and health were calculated based on the Social Accounting Matrix/Input-Output analysis. The costing exercises ensured that the expansion of education and health sectors can create decent work for women. 

These costing exercises were done under the high-level advisory committee hosted by the NPC. The findings were used by the Nepalese delegation in their presentation made in the &#xab; Technical Workshop on Care, Decent Work and Macroeconomic Policy &#xbb; hosted by the UN Women and ILO  in  Geneva. The findings were presented as a roadmap for addressing care service gaps and further commitment was expressed to prioritise investment in care services in the upcoming 16th national development plan of the government. The issue of full and productive employment and public investments in care was discussed as an agenda in Government&apos;s potential  commitment to be a pathfinder country under the Global Accelerator on Jobs and Social Protection Under Government of Nepal&#x2019;s leadership different research were undertaken, approved and validated through high-level National Steering Committee in collaboration with ILO and UN Women. Policy briefs with recommendations were formulated to address gender-based job segregation, costing gaps and economic returns of public investments in care sectors (education and health) in Nepal. The policy recommendations were shared at the high-level panel discussion and multistakeholder policy dialogue on 1st June and 2nd June, 2023. The assessments &#x2018;Gendered Employment Analysis and Policy Recommendations&#x2019; and &#x2018;Costing Gaps and Economic returns of Investments in Education and Health in Nepal&#x2019; were presented and raised awareness at the higher level among the policymakers to address the gaps in labour force participation. The GoN commtted to integrate women&#x2019;s decent employment and public investments in the care economy in the 16th periodic plan.</narrative>
   </description>
